  "articleBody": "When home renovations reach the drywall stage, sanding mistakes can easily show through your fresh interior paint. Here are six common missteps to watch for before hiring a crew.\n\n- Sanding compound before it fully hardens: Rushing wet mud tears the surface and leaves grooves that require another coat of mud.\n- Using coarse sandpaper: Heavy grit tears through the compound and scuffs the drywall face paper, creating rough textures visible through primer.\n- Skipping dust containment: Running sanders without floor protection or respirators sends fine respirable dust throughout your home's air.\n- Gouging the surrounding face paper: Rubbing past the compound boundary frays paper fibers, creating fuzzy patches that paint will not hide.\n- Forgetting surface cleanup: Painting over lingering drywall dust prevents primer from bonding, leading to peeling down the road.\n- Pressing too hard on high spots: Heavy hand pressure creates hollow divots rather than a flat, level transition across the studs.\n\nHow to Compare Your Options\nAsk your contractor about their dust-control plan, sanding grits, and cleanup methods before work begins. For expert finishing and drywall repairs across the Denver area, call Abraham Becerra at HH Remodeling LLC at 303-505-0866.",
